# Tracked mirror of vm23 root crontab lines installed by OPERATOR.sh.
# Merge idempotently on deploy. Hand-edits on vm23 should be copied back here.
# nsd-resign runs from etc/daily.local, not this crontab.

# The most important line in this file, and it is not a job.
#
# cron(8) runs with PATH=/bin:/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/sbin — no /usr/local/bin. Every
# interpreter this box actually uses lives there: ruby, ruby34, zsh. So every job
# below whose shebang is `#!/usr/bin/env ruby` or `#!/usr/bin/env zsh` had been
# failing at exec, once per scheduled tick, since it was installed. Measured
# 2026-08-12:
#
#   relayd-watchdog       every minute   env: ruby: No such file or directory
#   config-drift-check    every 15 min   /var/log/config_drift.log, same line, forever
#   renew-certs.sh        weekly         /var/log/cert-renewal.log, env: zsh: ...
#   nsd-resign            daily          /var/log/daily.out, ruby: not found
#
# Only resource_guard.sh ran, because it is the one job with a #!/bin/ksh shebang.
# Nothing alerted: each failure was one line in a log nobody reads, and the two
# jobs whose whole purpose is drift detection were themselves the drift.
#
# It stayed invisible because the *outcomes* looked fine. Certificates were valid
# (issued before the breakage, expiring 2026-10-29 with nothing to renew them),
# and DNSSEC signatures were valid (expiring 2026-08-30 with nothing to re-sign) —
# and no DS record is published, so the expiry could not bite yet. Publishing DS
# without this line would have taken every domain down on 2026-08-30.
PATH=/bin:/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/local/bin

# relayd-watchdog — restart relayd when unhealthy or backends stale
*/5 * * * * /usr/local/bin/relayd-watchdog

# config-drift-check — relayd/acme/NSD drift detection
*/15 * * * * /usr/local/bin/config-drift-check >> /var/log/config_drift.log 2>&1

# resource_guard — load shedding when vm23 is overloaded
*/5 * * * * /usr/local/bin/resource_guard.sh

# renew-certs — weekly ACME renewal (stage-1 installs renew-certs.sh)
0 2 * * 1 /usr/local/bin/renew-certs.sh >> /var/log/cert-renewal.log 2>&1

# uptime-check — public /up on every apps.yml host. Installed to
# /usr/local/bin so root cron does not exec the checkout. Wrapper lives
# in OPENBSD/usr/local/bin/uptime-check.sh.
*/5 * * * * /usr/local/bin/uptime-check.sh >> /var/log/uptime-check.log 2>&1

# prune-guests — nightly guest-row hygiene, because nothing runs the job queue.
#
# 04:20 and not daily.local, which is where this started. daily.local runs at the
# tail of /etc/daily, and /etc/daily has the box at load 4.12 by then — over
# PruneGuestUsersJob's own ceiling of 3.0, so the guard refused and the first
# night removed nothing at all. Its own slot is after daily(8) finishes, and the
# wrapper waits for the load to fall rather than skipping the night.
20 4 * * * /usr/local/bin/prune-guests.sh >> /var/log/prune-guests.log 2>&1

# drain-jobs — run the background queue for a few minutes each hour.
#
# No Solid Queue worker runs here: it needs its own process under Falcon, and
# with 60 MB free and 1032 MB of 1264 MB of swap in use a resident one per app
# does not fit. So `perform_later` meant never, and brgen was holding 178
# MessageExpirationJob rows — disappearing messages that had never disappeared.
#
# A bounded run does fit: 180 seconds drained 96 of 181 with load under 2. What
# it does not fix is latency, and ChannelBotReplyJob is the case that needs it —
# a bot answering an hour late is not answering. That wants a bigger box.
5 * * * * /usr/local/bin/drain-jobs.sh >> /var/log/drain-jobs.log 2>&1
